S3       WHY THE QUESTION IS ILL-POSED -- stated precisely
         The object is DEFINED as the fixed point: A3 and A6 make it orientable and
         minimal, which is what puts it at CS = 0 (xB020: orientation double covers
         are 2-torsion by construction; xB019: the squaring buys exactly this).
         A FIXED POINT CANNOT REPORT ON ITS OWN STABILISER.  Asking 'what does the
         object tell us about A6 or A7?' is not HARD -- IT IS EMPTY, because those
         are precisely the transformations under which the object does not move.
         SO THE WALLS ARE NOT OBSTACLES IN FRONT OF AN ANSWER.  THEY ARE THE SHAPE
         OF THE STABILISER, SEEN FROM INSIDE THE FIXED POINT.
         SCOPE, as the seal requires: this covers questions whose answer would have
         to be carried BY THE STABILISER acting on CS.  It does NOT cover arithmetic
         questions (S2's misses), nor anything about volume, H_1 or the trace field.

S4       THE EMERGENCE MECHANISM, AND ITS HONEST LIMIT
         If the information is in the ORBIT, work with the orbit.  THE EXHIBIT:
           the object realises ONE value of CS (0).
           the 2-torsion orbit realises TWO ({0, 1/4}) -- that is A5, the sisters.
           the FAMILY realises TWELVE: xB015's index 24*CS mod 12 is SURJECTIVE onto
             Z/12 over B1186's 112 members (with mirrors), against a 1.51% base rate.
         SO THE ORBIT DOES CARRY WHAT THE POINT DOES NOT.  That is the mechanism the
         owner asked for, and it is REAL: 1 -> 2 -> 12.

         AND THE LIMIT, DECLARED IN THE SEAL AND NOT SOFTENED NOW:
           xB016 already established that AN INDEX CANNOT FIX THE FREE ANCHOR.  sigma
           is a CONTINUOUS parameter of complex Chern-Simons and NOTHING COUPLES IT
           TO k (Witten eq. 2.2; Gukov sec 1.1).  The orbit's content is TOPOLOGICAL
           -- an integer mod 12 -- and a value is not an integer mod 12.
         => THE ORBIT HAS INFORMATION.  IT DOES NOT, SO FAR, HAVE THE ANSWER.
            'The orbit carries what the point does not' must NOT be read as 'the
            orbit carries what we need'.

S5       WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO
         It derives NO value.  It does not supply sigma, c, or any coupling.
         It does not replace B1234, which found the mirror upstream of eight walls;
           it EXTENDS that from one stabiliser element to the whole stabiliser, and
           gives the orbit-stabiliser count that makes it a theorem-shaped statement
           rather than a join.
         It does not cover the arithmetic negatives (S2's three misses).
         IT RE-POSES A QUESTION.  That is its entire content, and calling it more
         than that would be the exact failure this session has corrected four times.

S6       THE VERDICT
         THE SUM OF THE NEGATIVES HAS ONE SHAPE, AND IT IS COMPUTED, NOT ASSERTED:
           <A5, A6, A7> acts on CS as a group of order 4; the STABILISER of the
           object's value is exactly {A6, A7}; A5 is the orbit direction; and
           9 of 12 banked negatives are instances of 'the thing asked about is in
           the stabiliser'.
         WHY THE QUESTION IS ILL-POSED: it asks a fixed point to report on its own
           stabiliser.  Not hard -- EMPTY.
         THE MECHANISM: move to the orbit.  1 -> 2 -> 12, real and measured.
         THE LIMIT: the orbit's content is an INDEX, and xB016 closed the route from
           an index to the free anchor.  A DIAGNOSIS, NOT A DOOR.

         AXES HELD FIXED, NAMED AS THE RULE REQUIRES:
           - CS ONLY.  Volume, H_1 and the trace field are NOT analysed as group
             actions here, and the three arithmetic negatives fall outside.
           - the three bits only; the record's other torsor structure (B1083's C/P)
             is not re-derived.
           - the negatives audited are THIS SEAT's arcs plus cited older ones; the
             record's full negative inventory is NOT swept.

